                <h2 class="section-title">What Is a Copyright Objection?</h2>
                <div class="content-text what-is-definition">
                    
                    <p><strong>Definition:</strong> Formal discrepancy letter from Registrar of Copyrights identifying grounds why application cannot proceed. 30-day written reply required or automatic rejection.</p><p><strong>Two stages:</strong> (1) 30-day waiting period after filing: third-party can object. (2) Examination stage: Copyright Examiner reviews for completeness/originality/compliance, issues discrepancy letter.</p><p><strong>Third dimension:</strong> YouTube Content ID strikes / DMCA-style notices. Delhi HC Feb 2026 (TV9): strikes without actual litigation = S.60 groundless threats. Targets can seek Delhi HC declaration + injunction.</p>

                </div>
                <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:20px;">
                    <p><strong>Key Terms for Copyright Objection in Delhi:</strong></p>
                    <p><strong>Discrepancy Letter:</strong> Registrar's formal communication citing similarity, missing docs, third-party claims, or classification errors. Triggers 30-day reply clock.</p><p><strong>S.60 Groundless Threats:</strong> YouTube/digital strikes without actual court action = actionable. Delhi HC Feb 2026 TV9 ruling: declaration of non-infringement + injunction available.</p><p><strong>Copyright Journal:</strong> Official gazette. Rejected applications published. Public record. Difficult to reverse.</p><p><strong>S.52(1)(a)(iii):</strong> Fair dealing for reporting current events = complete defence. Confirmed in TV9 ruling for news footage use.</p>

                </div>
            </div>
